my name is Rob Riggle Schofield I work  for Wolfram and I am giving a talk this  afternoon called wolf sorry mobile CDF  deployment and by mobile what I mean  right now is iOS iPads and iPhones we  have done a fair amount of work on  Android support for all the stuff that  I'm going to be showing today but it's  quite a ways behind where our iOS  developments are so I'm not going to be  showing anything there so if you have  been to this conference the last several  years I have given a talk at this  conference with a similar title so I've  given this talk a few times what's  different this year from the previous  years so in the previous years I gave  this talk i was showing technology that  we have been working on that didn't  necessarily have a product to go along  with it this year i'm only going to be  showing products so there's three  different things that we're going to  look at one of them has already shipped  and two of them will be shipping very  soon so I'm fairly confident that next  year at this conference I won't be  giving the exact same talk and that  makes me feel a lot better so you can  now and very soon deploy your CDF  content to mobile devices using either  Wolfram's apps or your own app and I'm  going to explain how to do that so the  first thing I'm going to mention is  Wolfram cloud and I'm not going to go  too far into Wolfram cloud because there  is a separate talk that is dedicated  solely to Wolfram cloud at later this  afternoon I'll just highlight a few  interesting things about it so Wolfram  cloud is available now for iOS you can  go to the App Store and you can get it  you can download it for free you can log  in using your wolf own cloud account all  your documents will be there and you can  open them view them you can even edit  them so that's one of the the neat  things Wolfram cloud app has going for  it you can  eight and edit documents there are some  things about using the cloud which are  actually advantageous when you're  talking about interacting with mobile  device so if you're doing computations  that require a lot of CPU or a lot of  RAM doing in the cloud is probably  better and if you don't have the CPU  constantly spinning on your computations  the battery life on your mobile device  will last longer in those cases and it  has a very small download size since  it's basically just a really thin client  that calls out to the cloud to do a lot  of the heavy lifting so it does have a  few limitations that all I'll just  briefly mention it does require a  network connection since it's rico it  relies solely on the cloud for all the  document viewing and computation support  and the interactivity is a little bit  limited in that for example if you're  doing and manipulate or rotating  rotating a 3d graphic those sorts of  things do need to communicate to the  server over the network and that takes a  little bit of time so it's not quite as  a smooth experiences it would be if it  was all running locally on your device  so if you want to learn more about  Wolfram cloud there's a talk at  four-thirty today that my colleague Nick  will be giving you haven't met Nick he  is sort of grayish and not well defined  next is Wolfram player this is an app  that there was a lot of back and forth  about and we received a lot of feedback  from our users our users say said we  want a Wolfram player app for iOS and we  have decided that we are going to build  that we have been working very hard on  it and it will be released soon we hope  to release it by the end of this year so  definitely in time for next year's tech  conference so this is an app that we  plan to make available for free just as  the Wolfram player apps are free on  desktop systems and you will be able to  use it to view CDF documents step the if  you have static content like what I'm  looking at now you you can just view  that in the app for free if you have  Enterprise CDF documents that were  created from your actually don't know  exactly how that works but if you have  Enterprise CDF you know what that means  and you can create CDF documents that  you can distribute and people will be  able to use the free Wolfram player app  to do interactive things with those  documents so if you have dynamic content  you have manipulates you should be able  to do those for free with Wolfram player  app and then we're going to have an  optional in-app purchase for a  reasonable price to enable full dynamic  interactivity for arbitrary CDF  documents so stuff that doesn't that's  not enterprise CDF maybe stuff that you  just create on your desktop machine and  you want to carry it around with you in  your pocket or in your backpack that  will just be a an optional in-app  purchase to get dynamic interactivity  for those documents so Wolfram player is  a native app so it has a user interface  and user experience that's appropriate  for the platform whether that be I are  at iOS soon or Android a little bit  further off it has a Wolfram engine  embedded inside of the app so all the  computations are done locally and that  allows for a much better interactive  experience well from player supports all  the CDF content you would expect we have  nice typesetting and this is all we can  zoom into this and it still looks really  nice these aren't bitmaps we have to  dear affix  we have 3d graphics including  transparency and these are live you can  rotate them I'm just touching this with  my finger and kind of spinning it around  here and one of the neat things that  we've done on iOS is that we allow  tossing gestures so I can take this and  spin it really fast and just let it go  and it will kind of spin around for a  little while dynamic we have controls  and everything that has dynamic or all  of your the content that uses dynamic  will update appropriately when these  dynamic values change manipulate and  this is something that we had to do a  little bit differently than what we do  on desktop systems as I'm sure most of  you are familiar when you have a  manipulate command in desktop version of  Mathematica by default you get a bunch  of controls right above your content and  you can use your mouse to interact with  those controls if we place those above  the content on a touchscreen device your  hand is going to be blocking the content  that you're trying to look at so what we  do here by default and there's ways to  override this if you really want to we  tap on it to activate it and we slide up  some controls from the bottom of the  screen this one just has a single slider  and we can interact with a slider down  here without blocking the view of the  the content that we're working with and  then when you're done we can just tap  away in the controls slide off screen so  this version of Wolfram player is built  for touchscreens so on a desktop system  we have functions like mouse position  which don't make a lot of sense on an  iPad that I'm using here so we have a  new function called touch position that  allows you to do the same sorts of  things is mount you can with mouse  position on a desktop but it's a little  bit more flexible since you know right  now I'm not touching the screen there's  no touch  is the it just returns an empty list and  this is the thing with the green  background here so if I put a finger  down I get a point I put two fingers  down two points and I can keep loading  this up with a bunch of fingers here and  this works even while you know I'm  scrolling the document it's still  working so in addition to touch position  we have a function called gesture  handler which is very similar to what  event handler does on a desktop  mathematica one of the gestures we  support is a drag gesture so I just have  a simple example here where I turned off  I disabled a 3d rotation and i'm using  this drag gesture to adjust the radius  of the sphere here so you can do some  neat interactive things using gesture  handler and if if you have certain types  of controls like a vertical slider for  example if we pop up a vertical slider  from the bottom of the screen it's going  to cover half the screen so we have a  new option to manipulate called  touchscreen control placement for cases  like this where you can move the  controls over to the right side of the  screen or the left or the top or the  bottom you can specify where you want  the controls and so I have my vertical  slider over on the right here where it's  not taking up as much space as it would  at the bottom  okay so what are some of the limitations  we encounter when deploying CDF to iOS  so we have a relatively large download  size this is only going to affect you  once probably but it's a it's a few  hundred megabytes it does include the  full Wolfram engine and there's really  not any way to get around that so you're  not gonna be able to download it over  cellular connection but download it over  Wi-Fi and just keep it on your device  and you should be good so this is a  player app so the documents are  read-only this app doesn't allow editing  documents there's a handful of features  which are incomplete and will be  incomplete for the first you know for  the near future things like event  handler you know we're just not going to  support a lot of the types of events you  get on a desktop system tab you isn't  implemented there's certain cases of  locator that aren't implemented there's  limitations with input field it doesn't  support arbitrary 2d typeset content  it's just linear linear text input for  input field j link which requires a java  runtime is not available on iOS so we  have no j-link support and other  features which are built on top of  jailing things like database link these  are not going to be possible due to the  lack of G link there's some performance  considerations to to take into account  the older iOS devices in particular are  very have very small amount of RAM so  it's kind of a big deal just to get you  know Wolfram engine running at all with  such a small amount of RAM and you know  whatever computations you do on top of  that it's just going to you know add to  the need for memory so we try to handle  low memory situations gracefully  sometimes there's not a lot we can do  vos iOS will just sometimes abruptly  kill the app if it's using too much  memory but we try to avoid that whenever  we can the newer devices have a little  bit more RAM so it's less of an issue  with an ipad air or an ipad air to the  cpu is you know ferret fairly slow by  the standards of desktops and laptops  that we're used to working with i think  i mentioned a few years ago when i gave  this talk the mobile performance is  about five years behind where desktops  and laptops were at this point it's  actually slightly less they're probably  closer to three to four years behind I  got a new iphone 6s a few weeks ago and  I ran benchmarks on it and it's faster  than my macbook air from 2012 so they're  catching up which is which is  interesting other limitations the  Wolfram engine cannot currently be  restarted like you can in a desktop  version of mathematica you can go to the  menu and quit colonel we can't do that  so if for some reason that becomes  necessary you'll have to quit the entire  app and then start it up again so next  I'm going to talk about something called  CDF kit in CDF kit is an SDK that we are  making for iOS CDF kit is actually the  core of the Wolfram player app so if we  look at what's on the screen right now  the the dark bar across the top that's  the CDF player app and then everything  else on screen that's CDF kid and so  this is something that we use internally  for our apps and this is something that  we are going to be making available to  partners to build custom apps with as  well we already have a few partners who  are testing it at the moment and around  probably around the time when we release  the player app we will start making this  available more widely to more partners  so if you are interested in building a  custom app come see us and we'll we'll  see if we can work something out so  clients can embed cdf get into their iOS  apps and that will allow you to display  CDF content including interactive things  like 3d graphics dynamic manipulate you  can also use it to perform evaluations  using Wolfram language expressions so  even if you're not necessarily  displaying CDF content but you have a  chunk of Wolfram language code that you  just want to evaluate you can do that  even if you're not displaying anything  so there's a concise interface it's  fairly small fairly easy to wrap your  head around that you can call from your  objective c or swift app on iOS so this  isn't a this isn't a one hundred percent  solution there will be you will need to  write a little bit of code in  objective-c or Swift in order to take  advantage of the CDF kit SDK so there's  several different types of applications  that this will enable so in this  application the CDF content is basically  taking over the entire screen and we  have more content than displays in one  screen full so it Scrolls that's one  thing that we can do you can also have a  completely custom interface that just  has a tiny rectangle somewhere on the  screen that has CDF content inside of it  so what we're looking at here this is  the Wolfram player app that we're going  to be releasing soon and it is built  using CDF get we also have in  development we are working on a version  of the wolfram alpha app that uses CDF  kit and I don't know if and when that's  going to be available but hopefully  we'll have something like that and I'll  just give you a brief demonstration of  how that works  so this is my special version of the  wolfram alpha app and I have normal  wolframalpha output with this little  button here that I'm just going to tap  on and now what was once a static  Wolfram Alpha output is now interactive  and while I'm here I'm just going to  briefly show the Wolfram cloud app in  case you're not familiar with that these  are my cloud documents and I have the  same document that we were looking at in  the Wolfram player app and this takes a  couple seconds to open up here but this  is all running in the cloud this is a  very small app and there are features  that I can use to edit this document  which you'll you'll see a knicks talk if  you if you go to that so back in Wolfram  player I will just show you real quick  this was a file that was stored locally  on my iPad so I didn't need any network  connectivity to use that but we can also  access files stored in the wolfman cloud  and so this is that same list of files  that we just looked at in the Wolfram  cloud app but it's in Wolfram player and  I can open this same document and  everything just works as you would  expect

